    10	## Binding decisions and non-claims
    11	
    12	The extension has one byte ledger. Packet, HEV, SSH, DNS, relay, rekey, and
    13	reconnect owners cannot admit work from private budgets that ignore each other.
    14	Every admission reserves its worst permitted bounded storage before work starts.
    15	Release occurs exactly once only after the owning operation, queue, channel,
    16	session, lane, or transport is no longer reachable.
    17	
    18	The 25–30 MiB range is an engineering target, not an Apple memory guarantee.
    19	`os_proc_available_memory()` is current advisory evidence only: call it for
    20	each observation, never cache its value as future availability, and never turn
    21	unavailable or failed reads into zero. Jetsam is a failure, never control flow.
    22	
    23	An SSH advertised receive window is credit, not proof of eager allocation. The
    24	ledger still reserves the maximum local storage that the credit permits because
    25	the peer may consume it. Wire credit, resident bytes, and ledger reservation
    26	are separate metrics. Final watermarks, BDP caps, lane counts, rekey thresholds,
    27	relay caps, and reconnect overlap remain M3 tuning outputs.
    28	
    29	## Ledger model and units
    30	
    31	All quantities are unsigned bytes unless named otherwise. Counts/products use
    32	checked arithmetic. Overflow, a missing required ceiling, an unavailable owner
    33	count needed for exact charging, or a sum over its parent ceiling is a typed
    34	configuration/admission refusal; operands are not saturated into plausible
    35	values.
    36	
    37	```text
    38	current_i  = exactly observed bytes currently owned, when observable
    39	reserved_i = worst total/additional bytes promised to admitted work
    40	peak_i     = maximum current_i in the process generation
    41	charge_i   = max(current_i, reserved_i)
    42	
    43	ledgerCurrentBytes   = sum(current_i)
    44	ledgerReservedBytes  = sum(reserved_i)
    45	ledgerCommittedBytes = sum(charge_i)
    46	ledgerHeadroomBytes  = globalLedgerCeilingBytes - ledgerCommittedBytes
    47	```
    48	
    49	If exact current bytes are unavailable, the metric is
    50	`unavailable(reason, owner)` and charging uses the conservative live
    51	reservation, never zero. `physicalFootprintBytes` and its peak are independent
    52	process cross-checks, not added to the logical ledger; this avoids double
    53	counting and still catches native/allocator memory outside declared consumers.
    54	
    55	Every reservation records `reservationID`, consumer, owner, bytes, generation,
    56	admission monotonic time, lifetime, release event, and eventual release time and
    57	reason. IDs are opaque and bounded-cardinality. Missing is distinct from failed
    58	read; a failed reservation read is `unknown` and refuses admission.
    59	
    60	```text
    61	B_global = configured globalLedgerCeilingBytes
    62	B_base   = sum(non-overlap consumer charges)
    63	B_rekey  = sum(per-lane KEX emergency reservations)
    64	B_rc     = reconnect overlap reservation
    65	
    66	B_base + B_rekey + B_rc + B_untracked <= B_global
    67	```
    68	
    69	`B_untracked` explicitly covers code, allocator fragmentation, and native
    70	allocations not yet attributable. It is not an implicit remainder. Production
    71	authorization requires physical rows proving the complete process footprint.
