    84	
    85	## Lane pool
    86	
    87	One TCP connection creates cross-flow head-of-line blocking. The transport uses
    88	two to four SSH connections to the same host:
    89	
    90	- **Lane A — control:** relay exec, health, and latency-sensitive DNS/control;
    91	- **Lanes B and C — general:** ordinary TCP flows;
    92	- **Lane D — bulk:** optional/reserved capacity for new likely bulk flows.
    93	
    94	All lanes use the same verified host identity and profile credentials. Opening
    95	optional lanes is constrained by memory pressure and server limits.
    96	
    97	A flow is pinned to one lane until close. Live flows are never migrated because
    98	that would reorder the byte stream. New flows use congestion-aware assignment
    99	based on writable window, queued bytes, RTT/health, open-channel count, port,
   100	and best-effort destination metadata. Lane D cannot retroactively adopt a flow
   101	that later turns out to be an elephant.
   102	
   103	## Channel windows
   104	
   105	Initial candidate receive windows:
   106	
   107	- control and ordinary flows: 32–64 KiB;
   108	- bulk flows: calculated from a capped bandwidth-delay product and memory budget;
   109	- relay channel: small bounded control window plus enough credit for configured
   110	  maximum UDP frame bursts.
   111	
   112	An advertised receive window cannot be revoked. Under pressure the transport
   113	withholds `WINDOW_ADJUST` and gives smaller initial windows to new channels.
   114	Window policy is measured per lane and channel; it MUST NOT multiply a large
   115	default by the maximum session count.
   116	
   117	## Rekey and connection health
   118	
   119	- Rekey thresholds are configured by transferred bytes and elapsed time.
   120	- Client-initiated and server-initiated rekeys are supported.
   121	- New channel assignment may pause during key exchange, while existing channel
   122	  state remains valid.
   123	- A failed rekey fails only its lane first; the lane pool stops assigning new
   124	  flows there and re-establishes it without migrating existing flows.
   125	- Failure of lane A restarts relay/control capability and may temporarily enter
   126	  degraded mode; it does not silently leak UDP or DNS.
   127	- Keepalive distinguishes SSH liveness from application traffic and cannot be
   128	  the sole path-change detector.
   129	
   130	## QUIC policy
   131	
   132	`Auto` rejects new destination UDP/443 flows quickly when SSH lane loss/latency
   195	    Reasserting --> ConnectedDegraded: TCP + safe DNS restored
   196	    Reasserting --> Failed: retry policy exhausted
   197	    ConnectedFull --> Disconnecting: stop
   198	    ConnectedDegraded --> Disconnecting: stop
   199	    Failed --> Connecting: retry
   200	    Disconnecting --> Disconnected
   201	```
   202	
   203	During reconnect the provider sets `reasserting = true`. It stops assigning new
   204	flows to failed lanes and does not leak them to the physical route.
   205	
   206	Reconnect sequence:
   207	
   208	1. Observe path change, transport failure, sleep/wake, or viability loss.
   209	2. Preserve the last verified host identity and candidate IPs; invalidate lane
   210	   state and relay associations.
   211	3. Try cached endpoint IPs on the new physical path.
   212	4. If fresh resolution is needed, create an `NWConnection` with
   213	   `NWParameters.requiredInterface` set to the selected physical interface so
   214	   the lookup/connect cannot deadlock in the stale tunnel.
   215	5. Capture the actual new remote endpoint, rebuild its route exclusion, and
   216	   apply network settings atomically with the new transport.
   217	6. Restore mandatory lane A, safe DNS, packet plane, relay capability, and then
   218	   optional lanes.
   219	7. Set `reasserting = false` only after the advertised capability mode is usable.
   220	
   221	Old and new transports MUST NOT coexist with full channel windows beyond the
   222	explicit reconnect memory budget. At the critical memory watermark, release the
   223	old transport before opening the replacement.
